  Reason: score_above_high_threshold

Answer:
  Based on the provided context, a municipality can hold a public referendum when it has been decided after consultation with the central electoral authority which day of the week the referendum will take place and what questions and options will be put to those entitled to vote. The date for the referendum must be determined at least three months before the voting day. Additionally, if the municipal referendum coincides with another general election or national referendum, the color of the ballot papers used in the municipal referendum should differ from those used in other elections. Full representatives have the right to decide upon any additional regulations needed to conduct the referendum according to section 250 of the Act (2002). Furthermore, the municipality or region must notify its members about the matters decided under this act and about what else applies to the referendum. Section 8 states that voters who lose their ballots or do not receive them may obtain duplicate ballots if they request them. Finally, the central electoral authority has the right to reimbursement for costs incurred due to the municipal referendum.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

